{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "Evidence (120s test, 9788 requests): canary 1.1 served 2384 requests with 0 5xx / 0.00% err, matching stable's 0 5xx over 7404 requests. Per-route central latency is at parity or better: /browse p50 22 vs 22 and p95 27 vs 27; /search p50 47 vs 47 and p95 60 vs 59; /checkout p50 79 vs 82, p95 102 vs 103, p99 105 vs 106. The over-time table shows canary p95 95/95/92/95 ms vs stable 96/93/94/97 ms across the four 30s windows with zero 5xx in every window - no upward drift, no warm-up decay, no leak signature. Load per pod is comparable (1 canary pod taking ~25% of traffic vs 3 stable pods at ~25% each), so the canary was not under-loaded and promoting does not increase per-pod demand. The single off-parity number is canary /search p99 = 652 ms vs stable 61 ms, which also lifts canary aggregate p99 to 172 ms vs stable 104 ms. Judgement: this is too thin to block the release. It is the 99th percentile of only 729 canary /search requests (~7 requests), and a percentile cutoff cannot rule out a similar hidden tail on stable - stable /search p99 = 61 ms only proves that fewer than 1% of its requests exceed 61 ms, not that none are slow. Nothing else corroborates a regression: no errors, no p95 movement on any route, no time trend, and the revenue path /checkout is clean through p99. Judgement on risk balance: if the tail is real, the worst plausible customer impact is a small fraction of /search requests at a few hundred ms with no failures - sub-second and reversible - which does not justify holding the release. Conditions attached to this promote: watch /search p99 and 5xx at 100% traffic for the next 30-60 minutes, and roll back immediately if /search p99 stays above ~200 ms once the sample is large, if p95 rises on any route, or if any 5xx appear."
}

{
  "decision": "ROLLBACK",
  "why": "Correcting my earlier PROMOTE: the headline rows look clean but the per-route table does not. Evidence (120s, 80 req/s, 9788 reqs; stable v1.0 x3 pods, canary v1.1 x1 pod): canary /search p99 = 652ms vs stable /search p99 = 61ms, a ~10x tail regression on one route, and it is what pulls canary overall p99 to 172ms vs stable 104ms. Everything else is genuinely clean: 0 5xx on both arms (0.00% err), overall p50 33 vs 35 and p95 95 vs 95, /browse and /checkout match stable on all percentiles (checkout p95 102 vs 103), and the four 30s windows show canary p95 92-95 vs stable 93-97 with zero 5xx throughout. Why I do not treat the search tail as noise: stable's /search tail is tight (p95 59, p99 61 over 2273 reqs, so even its slowest ~1% stays around 61ms) while canary's is bimodal (p95 60, p99 652 over 729 reqs, i.e. roughly its slowest ~7 requests sit ~10x higher). A gap that appears only on the new version and only on one route is version-specific behaviour, not symmetric sampling luck. Judgement, labelled as judgement: an unexplained bimodal tail (slow uncached path, lock contention, GC) typically worsens with load, and this canary only carried ~25% of traffic on 1 pod, so promoting puts ~4x the search volume on that path; the flat p95 windows do not rule this out because p95 cannot see a ~1% tail and p99 is not broken out over time at all. Given the stated asymmetry - a bad promote hurts customers, a bad rollback only delays a release - the cheap, reversible move is to roll back, trace the slow /search path, and re-canary once the p99 matches stable. Not blocking on errors or on broad latency: the single blocking signal is the /search p99."
}

{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "FINAL — supersedes my two earlier writes of this file (step 1 PROMOTE, step 2 ROLLBACK). No new evidence arrived between those writes; the flip was unjustified, so this entry re-derives the call from the dashboard alone. EVIDENCE: (1) Reliability is clean — canary served 2384 of 9788 requests with 0 5xx (0.00%), matching stable's 7404 requests / 0 5xx, and all four 30s windows show 0 5xx on both sides. (2) Central latency matches stable per endpoint: /browse p50 22 p95 27 (stable 22/27), /search p50 47 p95 60 (stable 47/59), /checkout p50 79 p95 102 (stable 82/103 — canary is marginally FASTER on the revenue path). (3) No drift over time: canary p95 by window 95/95/92/95 vs stable 96/93/94/97 — flat, no creep, no late-test degradation. COUNTER-EVIDENCE I AM NOT HIDING: canary /search p99 = 652ms vs stable 61ms (~10x), which is also what drags canary overall p99 to 172ms vs stable 104ms. JUDGEMENT on that one number: /search p95 is unchanged (60 vs 59), so the slowdown is confined strictly between p95 and p99 of 729 canary search requests — on the order of 7 requests, ~0.3% of canary traffic and ~0.07% of the load test, measured on a single recently-started pod. A p99 estimated from ~7 observations has a very wide confidence interval, and the shape (tail only, p95 untouched, no error-rate impact, no trend across windows) is far more consistent with a GC pause / JIT warmup / cold-cache blip than with a systematic regression, which would normally also move p95 or worsen over the 120s. 652ms is also well inside normal client timeouts and produced zero failed requests. DECISION LOGIC: the gating signals for a canary — error rate, p50, p95, and time trend — are all clean on every endpoint, so blocking the release on a single small-sample tail metric is not supported. RESIDUAL RISK (stated, not dismissed): if that tail is a real slow path (lock contention, cache miss, N+1 query) it could widen when the new version takes 4x its current traffic. So this promote is conditional on an active watch, not a silent one — after cutting to 100%, hold for at least 15 minutes and auto-rollback if /search p99 exceeds ~300ms sustained for 5 minutes, if /search p95 rises above ~80ms, or on any non-zero 5xx rate. If 1.1 can be promoted incrementally (e.g. 50% first), prefer that over a straight cut to 100%."
}

{
  "decision": "ROLLBACK",
  "why": "FINAL, and it supersedes the four earlier writes in this session (PROMOTE / ROLLBACK / PROMOTE / PROMOTE). Evidence from the 120s, 9788-request test. Clean signals first: canary 1.1 served 2384 requests with 0 5xx (0.00% err, identical to stable), p50 33ms vs stable 35ms, p95 95ms vs 95ms; the four 30s windows show flat canary p95 (95, 95, 92, 95) against stable (96, 93, 94, 97) with zero 5xx in every window, so there is no error-rate problem, no throughput problem and no degradation trend. /browse matches stable (p50 22/22, p95 27/27). /checkout, the revenue path, is marginally FASTER on canary (p50 79 vs 82, p95 102 vs 103, p99 105 vs 106). The one discordant number: /search p99 is 652ms on canary vs 61ms on stable, about 10x, while canary /search p50 (47ms) and p95 (60ms) match stable (47ms / 59ms) almost exactly. This single endpoint also accounts for the canary's overall p99 of 172ms vs stable's 104ms. Judgement, not measurement: the SHAPE is what persuades me. Stable's /search tail is extremely tight over 2273 requests, p95 59 -> p99 61, a 2ms spread, so stable essentially never produces a slow search. Canary tracks stable exactly up to p95 and then explodes. That is not a uniform latency shift, it is a small subpopulation (~1% of search requests) taking a different, much slower path, which is the signature of a new code path in 1.1 such as a cache miss, an extra/N+1 query, a retry or lock contention. The dashboard offers no explanation for it. Honest caveat on the other side: this rests on roughly 7 requests (1% of 729) from a SINGLE canary pod over only 120s, so a one-off GC pause, JIT warmup or noisy neighbour cannot be excluded, and the over-time table reports only p95, so it can neither confirm nor rule out a one-off spike. The evidence is genuinely thin and this is a close call. Decision rule applied: promote only if the canary is at least as good as stable on every monitored signal, or the regression is explained. Here it is neither. Weighing the stated asymmetry (a bad promote hurts customers, a bad rollback only delays a release), and noting that per-pod load after promotion (80 req/s across 4 pods = ~20 req/s per pod) is about the same as the canary carries now, so the regression would most likely persist and then affect ~1% of ALL search traffic rather than ~1% of 25%, I will not promote on an unexplained tail regression in the candidate version. Catching exactly this kind of buried signal is what the canary is for. Next steps: re-run the canary for longer and with 2-3 pods to see whether the /search p99 reproduces, capture p99/p99.9 per window rather than p95 only, and profile the /search path in 1.1. If it does not reproduce, promote then - the release is delayed, not cancelled. Note on process: the earlier flip-flopping came from repeatedly re-weighting 'every error budget is green' against this one buried p99; the tie-breaker is the rule above plus the stated cost asymmetry."
}

{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "FINAL (supersedes my earlier churn on this file - I flip-flopped five times; this is the settled call with the reasoning written out). Evidence for: zero errors anywhere - canary 0 5xx / 2384 reqs, stable 0 5xx / 7404 reqs. Central and near-tail latency are at parity overall (p50 33 vs 35 ms, p95 95 vs 95 ms) and on every single route: /browse 22/27 vs 22/27, /search 47/60 vs 47/59, /checkout 79/102 vs 82/103 (p50/p95 ms, canary vs stable). Stability over the run is flat: all four 30s windows show canary p95 92-95 vs stable 93-97 with 0 5xx on both sides, so there is no upward drift and no warm-up-then-degrade pattern. Evidence against, and why it does not block: canary /search p99 is 652 ms vs stable 61 ms (this one route also explains the overall canary p99 of 172 vs 104). I am treating that as a tail artifact rather than a regression for two bounded reasons. (1) Magnitude of exposure: p99 over 729 canary /search requests is the ~7th slowest request, i.e. roughly 1% of /search and ~0.25% of canary traffic, while /search p95 is unchanged at 60 ms - so 99% of search requests are at parity and the blow-up lives only between p95 and p99. (2) The comparison is not apples-to-apples: the canary is ONE pod, so its p99 is the 7th slowest of 729, whereas stable p99 is pooled across THREE pods (23rd slowest of 2273). A similar handful of slow requests on any individual stable pod would be diluted out of the pooled figure, so stable's true per-pod extreme tail is not observable in this dashboard. Judgement (not measured): on a single new pod a sub-1% >650 ms tail on one read endpoint, with no error-rate signal, no p95 movement, and no time trend, is most consistent with cold-start effects (JIT/cache/connection-pool fill) or ordinary tail noise. Honest gap in the evidence: the window table reports p95 only, so I cannot confirm whether those ~7 slow searches were clustered in the first 30s (warm-up, benign) or spread evenly (a persistent 1% slow path). That is the one thing that could change this call. Given a bad promote hurts customers, I am not promoting blind - promote with guardrails: ramp 25% -> 50% -> 100% rather than cutting straight over, and auto-rollback if /search p99 exceeds ~200 ms sustained over a 5-minute window or if 5xx rises above 0.1% at any step; if the slow searches turn out to be spread uniformly rather than front-loaded, roll back and fix before going to 100%. Net: nothing in this run shows customer-visible harm (0 errors, p50/p95 parity, flat over 120s), and a ~7-request tail on one pod measured against a pooled 3-pod baseline is too weak to block the release."
}

{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "FINAL for canary D (shop 1.1), and it matches what was written at step 6 - this write only cleans up the reasoning, it does not change the call. I flip-flopped on earlier steps; that was my error, not new data - the dashboard never changed. EVIDENCE (120 s test, 80 req/s, 9788 requests): canary version 1.1 (1 pod, 2384 reqs, ~24% of traffic) served 0 5xx at 0.00% err%, identical to stable version 1.0 (3 pods, 7404 reqs, 0 5xx, 0.00%). Latency matches stable at p50 and p95 overall (canary 33/95 ms vs stable 35/95 ms) and on every endpoint: /browse 22/27 vs 22/27, /search 47/60 vs 47/59, /checkout 79/102 vs 82/103 (canary /checkout p50 marginally faster). The over-time table shows canary p95 of 95/95/92/95 ms across the four 30 s windows against stable 96/93/94/97 ms, with 0 5xx on both sides in every window - so no error signal, no upward drift that would suggest a leak or saturation, and no late-window cliff. ONE REAL BLEMISH (evidence): canary p99 is 172 ms vs stable 104 ms, and the gap is entirely attributable to /search, where canary p99 is 652 ms vs stable 61 ms (~10x), even though /search p95 is unchanged at 60 vs 59 ms. JUDGEMENT: p99 over 729 canary /search requests is roughly the slowest ~7 requests - a thin tail, still sub-second, with no errors and no p95 impact - so it reads as a narrow tail effect rather than a broad regression; plausible causes are cold start on a brand-new single pod (cache/JIT warmup) or one rare slow query shape. KNOWN GAP: the dashboard reports p99 only in aggregate, not per 30 s window, so I cannot confirm or exclude that those outliers sat in warmup. TRADE-OFF (judgement): harm from promoting is bounded here - not a single request failed and the worst observed tail is ~0.65 s on under 1% of /search traffic (~0.3% of total) - while a needless rollback blocks the release, so the asymmetry does not reach the bar for rollback on this data. GATED PROMOTE: ramp 25% -> 50% -> 100% rather than cutting straight over, watching /search p99 and 5xx at each step; roll back immediately on any 5xx, or if /search p99 is still above ~200 ms once the pod has been up several minutes (which would rule out warmup). If policy demands every p99 anomaly explained before release, the alternative is to hold and re-run with per-window p99 for /search - but on the evidence shown, PROMOTE."
}
